    "title": "perf(file): avoid opening files for file_size",
    "user": {
      "login": "yongster",
      "id": 76621090,
      "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjc2NjIxMDkw",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/76621090?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/yongster",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/following{/other_user}",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/gists{/gist_id}",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/starred{/owner}{/repo}",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/yongster/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "body": "`file_size()` now retrieves the size of whole-file references directly from object metadata instead of loading each file through `DaftFile::load()`.\r\n\r\nThis avoids the unnecessary `supports_range()` probe and reader construction that previously happened after the file size had already been fetched. The function is now an async scalar UDF, allowing independent size lookups in a batch to run concurrently.\r\n\r\nByte-range file references retain the existing load behavior so their returned size semantics are unchanged.\r\n\r\nAdded coverage for multiple file inputs and null propagation.\r\n\r\nFixes #7480 \r\n\r\n- `cargo check -p daft-file`\r\n- `make build`\r\n- `DAFT_RUNNER=native make test EXTRA_ARGS='-v tests/file/test_file_basics.py -k filesize'`\r\n",
    "created_at": "2026-09-07T13:47:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-11T07:37:07Z",
    "closed_at": "2026-09-11T07:37:07Z",
